WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.385 --> 00:00:01.520
A little bit about me...

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:05.985
You might not know who I am.
I grew up in this place called Dubai.

00:00:05.985 --> 00:00:09.865
It's not part of Illinois. (I was once asked that).

00:00:09.865 --> 00:00:12.695
It's actually part of the Middle East.

00:00:12.695 --> 00:00:14.735
It's in this country called the United Arab Emirates.

00:00:15.685 --> 00:00:20.765
This is what it looked like when I was 10.
So now you know how old I am.

00:00:22.005 --> 00:00:25.035
Just stare at the screen for a minute...

00:00:25.395 --> 00:00:27.615
This is 1991.

00:00:28.035 --> 00:00:29.715
This is 2005.

00:00:30.445 --> 00:00:34.615
Now look at those 4 buildings right there. Those haven't changed.

00:00:36.725 --> 00:00:38.235
Woooah.

00:00:39.025 --> 00:00:41.865
Hopefully you're drinking and this looks really cool.

00:00:42.305 --> 00:00:45.495
I'll just go back and forth a few times.

00:00:45.495 --> 00:00:47.525
So why am I showing this to you?

00:00:47.595 --> 00:00:49.935
The reason I'm showing this to you is because

00:00:50.065 --> 00:00:55.435
in a very short span of time we can actually transform our environment really really quickly.

00:00:55.435 --> 00:01:00.025
This is amazing technology, amazing innovation, amazing engineering.

00:01:00.625 --> 00:01:03.375
It's also to me, growing up here,

00:01:05.365 --> 00:01:08.735
it's very difficult to look at this

00:01:08.735 --> 00:01:11.705
and sort of wonder "where did the desert go?"

00:01:11.705 --> 00:01:14.865
What happened to my landscape?

00:01:15.105 --> 00:01:18.105
They're building ski resorts in the middle of the desert.

00:01:18.155 --> 00:01:20.900
There are 3 so far. More designed to be built.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:26.120
This is what it's going to look like.
So it's sort of an oil to ice conversion.

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:32.880
This is The Palm Resort. Everyone gets beach access.

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:35.560
Who doesn't love the beach?

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:39.380
This is a photograph that I took. And this was my last visit to the UAE.

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:41.920
It's very difficult for me to actually go back

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:45.780
because it doesn't feel or look like the place I grew up in.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:52.000
So this is a photograph that I took from the shore
of a ship dumping sand into the ocean to make land.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:56.760
And this is how the shoreline has transformed over time.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:00.920
This is 1973, 1990, and 2006

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:05.040
So do you see the palm islands and the world islands? It's pretty amazing, right?

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:10.600
So in a very very short period of time, the entire environment has completely transformed itself.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.640
And so this is where I grew up.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.140
This is my background.

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:17.780
So I came to the United States in 2000.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:19.260
And I was at University of Illinois Urbana–Champaign.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:20.640
There are some alums here,

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.300
we went  to school together, 
and they found me here.

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.560
Amazing.

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:29.135
I studied physics and math.

00:02:29.135 --> 00:02:31.795
And i was like, alright, physics is amazing.

00:02:32.025 --> 00:02:34.600
But I really wanted to work on climate change.

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:36.080
I really wanted to work on sustainability.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.680
The United Arab Emirates has
a very large environmental footprint.

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:42.320
So does the United States.

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:45.960
And so the year that I started by PhD was 2004.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:47.860
So I'm going to kind of give you the story of my life.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:50.900
Then I'm going to embed that
into the story of the work that I'm doing.

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:55.840
So in 2004, the same month that I started my PhD,

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:58.860
this paper came out in Science magazine
by Pacala and Socolow.

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:01.780
And the first sentence in their paper was

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:06.540
Alright humanity already has all of the solutions
we need to solve the climate problem.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:09.020
We don't have to wait. We have the technology we need.

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:14.140
We can solve it today.
Humanity already has all these solutions.

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:16.800
Then... why haven't we done anything about it?

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:20.340
We still have this problem happening right now.

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:22.800
So what do they say in their paper?

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.780
So this was a kind of back of the envelope exercise...

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:29.280
They said, if we want to stabilize emissions,

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:35.125
and then we want to stabilize concentration,
let's just think of it in terms of a variety of technologies.

00:03:35.125 --> 00:03:36.800
A variety of things we can do.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:41.605
So they said alright imagine each
of these technologies providing a wedge.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:44.640
A sort of one gigaton of carbon decrease per year

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:46.860
everything from efficiency and conservation

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:50.340
renewable electricity, carbon capture and storage,

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:56.900
reforestation, nuclear energy, biomass, fuel switching, so on and so forth. This is just to stabilize emissions.

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:02.000
And then after that we need to actually
decrease our emissions to net removal.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:05.840
They put forward this entire portfolio.

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:09.020
I said, great. Why aren't we doing that stuff?

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:13.440
What they also said in their paper is
improvements in efficiency and conservation

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:16.420
offer the greatest potential to solve this problem.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:19.900
So i started to dedicating my career from that point on

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:25.420
to figuring out why is it that we don't
incorporate efficiency and conservation in our life?

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:28.320
And I've been focusing primarily in the United States.

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:31.100
So what's been going on?

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:34.840
As you can tell our carbon dioxide concentration

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:37.840
which is basically how much carbon dioxide
there is in the atmosphere

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:39.660
has been going steadily up.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:43.620
So it just keeps increasing every year.
So every year that I teach my course

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:46.440
I have to keep updating the numbers.
They keep going up!

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:49.920
Every time I teach that one section of my class

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:55.040
it's like internal pain. I have to go home
and have myself a nice gin &amp; tonic at the end of the day.

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:58.100
How do we solve this?

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:03.280
Then when Al Gore came up with the Inconvenient Truth

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:06.160
he was trying to keep it under 400 parts per million.

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:09.980
So just as per cent is out of a hundred,
parts per million is out of a million.

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:14.940
So we're trying to keep this at below doubling.
And we just passed 400 parts per million.

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:18.920
So that is the challenge that we're faced with today.

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:21.515
So how do you visualize this in terms of temperature?

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:25.560
This is a beautiful graphic that Ed Hawkins did.
It's basically a color map.

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:30.820
Looking at the average temperature in the United States.
And each stripe is one year.

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:36.100
As you can probably tell (you may need to squint from the back of the room), it's going from bluish to reddish.

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:38.420
Folks in the back of the room, do you see that?

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:43.480
So that's what's happening
with our average annual temperature.

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:46.180
We're getting warmer.

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:52.320
So in trying to figure out why we don't act,
I asked 700 people across the United States,

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:56.045
What is the single most important problem
for you today?

00:05:56.045 --> 00:05:58.340
What is the most important problem
for you in the future?

00:05:58.340 --> 00:06:03.080
And what do think is the world's
biggest problem today and  in the future?

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:05.840
And here's the data. This data was collected in 2010.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:11.300
So the #1 problem that America faces today
is the economy. Alright?

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:15.780
The #1 problem that the world faces in the future
is climate change.

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:19.240
So the problem there is that the future
always happens in the future!

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:22.480
How do we speed that shit up?

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:26.100
So how do we make the future happen today?

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:29.440
And that's why I'm really interested
in stories and storytelling.

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:33.300
I'm interested in how we compress time?

00:06:33.565 --> 00:06:36.615
How do we imagine what
the future's going to look like, today?

